<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-10">
    <title>Week 11</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            <alert>We are switching textbooks! Section numbers now
                   refer to Taylor and Wheeler.
            </alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading Quiz 15 Covers 1.1-1.4 and 2.1,2.2,2.6,2.7</alert>
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-chap1">
        <title>Spacetime Physics Chapter 1</title>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Chapter 1 is about getting acquainted with the idea of
                        <term>spacetime.</term> In special relativity, rather than
                        thinking of space and time as separate, they are merged in
                        a single entity.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The chapter starts with a geometric analogy to make the ideas
                        a bit more palatable. Just like the angle between magnetic North
                        and stellar North is small, the effects of special relativity are
                        small because the speed of light is so large. Some of the
                        consequences of special relativity seem bizarre at first sight,
                        but we'll get used to them.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        An important thing for this chapter: although we will talk about
                        things that are different for different observers, the unification
                        of space and time in SR leads to the concept of the <term>spacetime
                        interval</term>, a quantity all observers agree on.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-chapt2">
        <title>Spacetime Physics Chapter 2</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Chapter 2 is about understanding what an inertial reference frames is. 
                        Section 2.2 provides a definition, and section 2.3 emphasizes that
                        inertial frames are actually local because of gravity.
                        SR does not deal with gravity, so we won't have to worry
                        about this: our inertial frames are still infinite.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A new thing from special relativity, which we will explore
                        next week, is that the speed of light is also an invariant
                        quantity. This is impossible in Newtonian physics, and this
                        descovery will require us updating our concepts of space and time.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Section 2.6 introduces a very concrete visualization for an
                        inertial reference frame, and section 2.7 makes the distinction
                        between seeing and observing. As we move forward, it is important
                        to keep in mind that the effects of special relativity are real,
                        not visual tricks or measurement error, but rather a consequence
                        of the fact that how you <q>slice</q> spacetime into space and
                        time turns out to depend on your state of motion.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </subsection>
</section>